export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"bimcraft","addon":"scanbim","name":"Scan to BIM","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"scan-to-bim","scope":"addon:scanbim","title":"Scan to BIM: walls, floors and openings from a point cloud","summary":"Open a point cloud in BimCraft and get proposed floors, walls, doors and windows, each with how far it sits from the scanned points. You accept, edit or reject them.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["scan","point cloud","scan to bim","walls","doors","windows","floors","phone scan","laser scan","e57","las","ply","proposals","deviation"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-does","text":"What it does","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"how-far-from-the-points","text":"How far from the points","level":2},{"id":"what-it-cannot-know","text":"What it cannot know","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-does\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EScan to BIM reads a point cloud you have already opened and proposes a first model of the building: the floors, the walls on each floor (with thickness and height), the doors and windows in them, and a floor for every room the walls close. Nothing is drawn until you accept. It works in BimCraft, and it needs the Point clouds add-on.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a model in BimCraft, switch on \u003Cstrong\u003EPoint clouds\u003C/strong\u003E in Settings, General, Add-ons, and build your cloud there (LAS, LAZ, E57, PLY, PCD, PTX, XYZ or PTS). Use the Point clouds tab to clean stray points first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESwitch on \u003Cstrong\u003EScan to BIM\u003C/strong\u003E and open its tab. It says which cloud is open and whether the cloud lines up with the model or is centred on it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFind the floors.\u003C/strong\u003E The add-on looks at the heights of all the points. Where many points lie on one level it finds a flat surface. The lowest is a floor. A surface at least 1.8 m above it is its ceiling. If it guessed wrong, type the floor and ceiling heights.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose a floor and press \u003Cstrong\u003EPropose\u003C/strong\u003E. For walls it cuts a slice 1.2 m over the floor and finds the long thin runs of points, as the plan-trace in BimCraft&#039;s Scan menu does. For each wall it reads the rest of the storey&#039;s points to get its height and, when both faces were scanned, its thickness. Doors and windows are gaps in the wall&#039;s own points.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the table. Untick what you do not want, type a new thickness, height, width or sill, then press \u003Cstrong\u003EShow on the plan\u003C/strong\u003E to see the proposals on the level, or \u003Cstrong\u003EAccept ticked\u003C/strong\u003E to draw them. Accepting is one undo step. \u003Cstrong\u003EReject all\u003C/strong\u003E drops everything.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-far-from-the-points\"\u003EHow far from the points\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery proposal carries a number in millimetres: how far it sits from the scanned points, with the number of points it was measured on.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Ewall\u003C/strong\u003E: the spread of the points that lie on its faces (within 60 mm), around the face. Points of other walls meeting it are not counted.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Edoor or window\u003C/strong\u003E: the gap between each proposed edge and the nearest point beside it, and the number of points left inside the opening.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Efloor\u003C/strong\u003E: the spread of the floor points, away from the walls, around the floor level.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA small number says the points agree with the proposal. It does not say the building is that size: a scan has its own error, and the units and scale of the file are the file&#039;s.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-cannot-know\"\u003EWhat it cannot know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA wall scanned from one side shows one face, so its thickness is \u003Cstrong\u003Eassumed\u003C/strong\u003E (the figure you type, 150 mm to start). A wall scanned from both sides has its thickness \u003Cstrong\u003Emeasured\u003C/strong\u003E. Each wall says which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOnly rectilinear rooms are read: walls that are parallel to one of two perpendicular directions. A curved or diagonal wall is not found.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA shadow in the scan, where nothing could see, looks like an opening. Check each one against the building.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFloor and ceiling are told apart by their distance (1.8 m or more). A mezzanine or a very low room can fool it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt has been tried on made-up clouds of a known room, and the error of each number is in the test log. A real phone scan with a tape-measured wall is still to be checked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStairs, curved or sloped walls, columns, roofs and ceilings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMatching proposals to walls already in the model (proposals are new walls on the level).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWindow types and door swings: doors and windows are placed as plain openings you then change.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What it does Scan to BIM reads a point cloud you have already opened and proposes a first model of the building: the floors, the walls on each floor (with thickness and height), the doors and windows in them, and a floor for every room the walls close. Nothing is drawn until you accept. It works in BimCraft, and it needs the Point clouds add-on. Steps Open a model in BimCraft, switch on Point clouds in Settings, General, Add-ons, and build your cloud there (LAS, LAZ, E57, PLY, PCD, PTX, XYZ or PTS). Use the Point clouds tab to clean stray points first. Switch on Scan to BIM and open its tab. It says which cloud is open and whether the cloud lines up with the model or is centred on it. Find the floors. The add-on looks at the heights of all the points. Where many points lie on one level it finds a flat surface. The lowest is a floor. A surface at least 1.8 m above it is its ceiling. If it guessed wrong, type the floor and ceiling heights. Choose a floor and press Propose. For walls it cuts a slice 1.2 m over the floor and finds the long thin runs of points, as the plan-trace in BimCraft's Scan menu does. For each wall it reads the rest of the storey's points to get its height and, when both faces were scanned, its thickness. Doors and windows are gaps in the wall's own points. Look at the table. Untick what you do not want, type a new thickness, height, width or sill, then press Show on the plan to see the proposals on the level, or Accept ticked to draw them. Accepting is one undo step. Reject all drops everything. How far from the points Every proposal carries a number in millimetres: how far it sits from the scanned points, with the number of points it was measured on. A wall: the spread of the points that lie on its faces (within 60 mm), around the face. Points of other walls meeting it are not counted. A door or window: the gap between each proposed edge and the nearest point beside it, and the number of points left inside the opening. A floor: the spread of the floor points, away from the walls, around the floor level. A small number says the points agree with the proposal. It does not say the building is that size: a scan has its own error, and the units and scale of the file are the file's. What it cannot know A wall scanned from one side shows one face, so its thickness is assumed (the figure you type, 150 mm to start). A wall scanned from both sides has its thickness measured. Each wall says which. Only rectilinear rooms are read: walls that are parallel to one of two perpendicular directions. A curved or diagonal wall is not found. A shadow in the scan, where nothing could see, looks like an opening. Check each one against the building. Floor and ceiling are told apart by their distance (1.8 m or more). A mezzanine or a very low room can fool it. It has been tried on made-up clouds of a known room, and the error of each number is in the test log. A real phone scan with a tape-measured wall is still to be checked. Not built yet Stairs, curved or sloped walls, columns, roofs and ceilings. Matching proposals to walls already in the model (proposals are new walls on the level). Window types and door swings: doors and windows are placed as plain openings you then change."}]};
